= JVM: note/sayfh/printfh ops, NQP_CODE_WHY frame verdicts, stage0 refresh
note prints a line on stderr; printfh/sayfh write a string or a line through a handle's writable,
registered in the JVM op table and encoded through the classlib registry. NQP_CODE_WHY prints each
block's frame verdict with its inputs (stdout, never exported to make). Bare implicit magicals
($/ $! $_ $¢) kept as bare slots by an early re-formation no longer force a frame. stage0 is
regenerated from the current stage2 so the bootstrap compiler knows the new ops and env-gated
traces in the compiler sources can use nqp::note.
- 88bf01a45 JVM: add the note, sayfh and printfh ops
- e622bbd9f Code engine: NQP_CODE_WHY also prints each block's frame verdict with its inputs
- 1865ad4e7 Code engine: bare implicit magicals do not force a frame
- 48f1e0147 Bootstrap refresh: stage0 from the current stage2

= jesp diamond 7: frame-free across languages, by language name
# moved: 1b9cde8d1 (NqpDispatch.kt only) pulled ahead of 263665446 (NqpOps.java only) and
# b013a8757 (CompilationUnit.kt only) to sit with the diamond it corrects.
The encoder records per block whether any op reads the current language off the frame (%hll_ops)
as bit 1 of the wire's frame word; NqpDispatch's entry decisions become needsFrame || (!hllFree &&
!sameHll); getattr boxes a native slot read with the block's own unit. Only a different language
NAME crosses frame-free: a bootstrap's two `nqp` configs (compiler and compilee) stay framed, so
stage2's NQPHLL no longer binds to stage1's setting. JESP_HLLFREE=0 keeps diamond 5's rule.
- c36996b3f jesp diamond 7: frame-free across languages when the block reads no current language
- 1b9cde8d1 jesp diamond 7: only a different language NAME crosses frame-free

= jesp: prologue and return boundaries go; arguments read from the frame
# moved: 5d8157754 (NqpOps.java, NqpProgramBuilder.java, NqpRootNode.java) pulled ahead of
# b013a8757 (CompilationUnit.kt only).
storeReturnTyped, posparam and truthy are plain inlinable code; the caller-less return, the
optional and converting parameter fetches and an object's boolification stay behind a boundary.
FlatArgs reads the frame's own argument array when the arity check answered the frame's own
callsite (nothing flattened), so the array no longer escapes through tc.flatArgs and escape
analysis virtualizes it in the inlined direct-road callees.
- 263665446 jesp: the prologue and return boundaries go
- 5d8157754 jesp: the argument array is read from the frame, not parked on the thread context

